WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.060
The Linux GPU stack includes several layers that have to line up.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.615
Kernel modules provide low-level driver functionality inside the running kernel.

00:00:07.615 --> 00:00:09.680
Firmware may be required by the hardware.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.000
User-space driver libraries provide interfaces that applications and runtimes call.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.420
Display drivers may support monitors and desktops,

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.260
while compute runtimes support GPU-accelerated workloads.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.755
Package managers install and update many of these pieces,

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:25.580
but AI workloads often depend on more than one package.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.823
Kernel headers may be needed to build a driver module.

00:00:27.823 --> 00:00:31.696
DKMS, at a high level, helps rebuild kernel modules when kernels change.

00:00:31.696 --> 00:00:35.140
Device nodes expose GPU hardware to user-space processes.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:39.300
Permissions determine which users, services, and containers can access those devices.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.780
A working desktop display does not automatically mean compute workloads are ready.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.780
Likewise, a headless inference server may not need a full desktop stack,

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.630
but it still needs the right kernel support, firmware, user-space libraries,

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:51.980
and runtime interfaces.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.550
The exact package names vary by distribution and vendor,

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.725
but the architectural responsibilities are similar.

00:00:57.725 --> 00:01:00.375
Understanding these layers makes troubleshooting less mysterious.

00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:03.944
If the GPU is not visible, look at hardware detection and kernel modules.

00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:06.660
If the driver is loaded but an AI library fails,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.540
look at user-space libraries and runtime compatibility.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.480
If containers cannot see the GPU, look at device exposure,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.340
container runtime configuration, and permissions.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.300
AI acceleration depends on the complete stack.
